From 4fbf1b8e7098ff4f4a9d6592a1873af851ce8ed9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 19:46:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] perf(parser): answer the autolink pre-flight from
 root-scan trigger offsets

With GFM autolinks on, the aarch64 root scan that already reads the
whole body for NUL and the first `]:` now also records the offsets of
the autolink pre-flight's trigger bytes: every `@`, and every `:` or `w`
that a `.` or `/` follows. That is eight more vector operations per 16
bytes and one branch per 64 bytes that stays shared with the NUL and
`]:` test. After the first `]:` the scan keeps going for NUL and
triggers instead of handing the rest of the body to `memchr`.

`parse_inline_block` then answers `may_contain_autolink` for content
that is a slice of the root body by visiting only the offsets inside
its range, with the old per-position rules on the content's own bytes:
`//` must follow inside the content, the `](scheme` check looks back no
further than the content start, and a `www.` counts only when all four
bytes are inside. Content holding an `@` goes to the full pass, which
also settles `mailto:`/`xmpp:`. A cursor makes source-order lookups
constant time. Sub-sources the parser copies (block quotes, most list
items, escaped table cells, comment-stripped lines), NUL-normalized
documents, profiles without link references or footnotes, and other
targets keep the separate pass.

The answer equals the full pass flag for flag: test builds assert it
for every block they parse (on every target, with the portable
byte-wise collector off aarch64), and a differential test compares AST
Debug output and HTML with and without the offsets over the spec
fixtures, two frozen corpora, needles split across block boundaries
and generated documents.

Experimental: performance is to be measured with the paired harness.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
