From cb73e496a462f4460f528c0404f8a90060ce5c12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 21:24:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] perf(parser): record autolink triggers lazily, per block,
 with a density bound

The paired measurement of the first commit showed a broad win, parse
1.010, and one clear loser: typescript-handbook-compiler-options, parse
0.870. That document is 54 KB of raw HTML tables with only 2 trigger
bytes and 834 bytes of inline content outside containers. The loss was
the extra vector work in the root scan over every byte of a body whose
inline blocks never needed it. A density bound alone would not reach it.

The root scan and normalization are back to main. The root parser
instead records trigger offsets with the same NEON test, now in
`gfm_autolink/triggers/collect.rs`, only for bytes that blocks ask for:

- A block that starts inside the recorded run, or at most 64 bytes past
  it, extends the run to its end. It reads ahead to the next 512-byte
  boundary, so the next blocks of a stretch only look up offsets.
- A block past a stretch no block asked for (code, raw HTML) starts a
  new run of exactly its own bytes, which is what the full pass would
  have read.
- Content before the current run takes the full pass.

Every byte is recorded at most once. The recorded bytes exceed what the
full pass reads only by the gaps between blocks and one read-ahead per
stretch. With the root scan no longer involved, the index also covers
profiles without link references or footnotes and NUL-normalized
documents.

The record stops for good once it holds more than 32 offsets plus 16
per KB recorded. The broad corpus averages about 1 per KB and peaks at
7.4 per KB. Every later block then takes the full pass.

Exactness is argued as before and checked the same way. New tests cover
the bound exactly at and one past its limit, a dense prefix before
sparse content, a sparse prefix before a dense block, isolated blocks
that record only their own bytes, runs across gaps and read-ahead, and
parser-level documents around the bound in every container.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
